            <ArticleTitle>Cyborgian Material Design for Solar Fuel Production: The Emerging Photosynthetic Biohybrid Systems.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>476-481</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.accounts.6b00483</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Photosynthetic biohybrid systems (PBSs) combine the strengths of inorganic materials and biological catalysts by exploiting semiconductor broadband light absorption to capture solar energy and subsequently transform it into valuable CO2-derived chemicals by taking advantage of the metabolic pathways in living organisms. In this work, we first traverse through a brief history of recent PBSs, demonstrating the modularity and diversity of possible architectures to rival and, in many cases, surpass the performance of chemistry or biology alone before envisioning the future of these hybrid systems, opportunities for improvement, and its role in sustainable living here on earth and beyond.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
